"""
Speed sweep across the three flow-driven convection factors, with a cylinder cooling
through its own coefficient.

The wind ramps from rest to 20 m/s over the run while a hot body sits in air at 25 °C, so
each element's `h` is swept across the branches of its correlation in one transient:

- `cyl` covers the natural-convection plateau at rest, the Nakai–Okazaki slug-flow branch
  and both Churchill–Bernstein forms. It carries the only thermal mass in the model, so it
  also checks the element inside a real cooling loop rather than between two ideal sources.
  Its geometry is the ceramic disk resistor of upstream's `CeramicDiskResistor_Test`
  (D = 151 mm, L = 889 mm).
- `windward` and `leeward` are the same wall, facing south and north, in a wind blowing
  due north. They share every parameter, so `leeward.h` should be exactly half
  `windward.h` throughout — the windward/leeward factor is the only thing that differs.
- `plate` sweeps the turbulent flat-plate correlation. Below Re ≈ 5·10⁵ it is out of the
  range the correlation is fitted over; `plate.Re` shows where the run crosses it.

The hot side is held at 150 °C except for `cyl`, which cools from there.
"""
test component ConvectionSpeedSweep
  wind = BlockComponents.Sources.Ramp(height = 20.0, duration = 200.0, offset = 0.0, start_time = 0.0)
  calm = BlockComponents.Sources.Constant(k = 0.0)

  hot = ThermalComponents.Sources.FixedTemperature(T = 423.15)
  air = ThermalComponents.Sources.FixedTemperature(T = 298.15)

  body = ThermalComponents.Components.HeatCapacitor(C = 5e4, T0 = 423.15)
  cyl = ConvectionHorizontalCylinder(D = 0.151, L = 0.889)

  plate = PlateForcedConvection(L = 2.0, A_plate = 6.0)

  windward = ExternalConvection(Surf = 10.0, Norm = [0, -1, 0])
  leeward = ExternalConvection(Surf = 10.0, Norm = [0, 1, 0])
relations
  connect(body.port, cyl.solid)
  connect(cyl.fluid, air.port)
  connect(wind.y, cyl.u_inf)

  connect(hot.port, plate.solid)
  connect(plate.fluid, air.port)
  connect(wind.y, plate.AirSpeed)

  connect(hot.port, windward.solid, leeward.solid)
  connect(windward.fluid, leeward.fluid, air.port)
  connect(wind.y, windward.WindVector_y, leeward.WindVector_y)
  connect(calm.y, windward.WindVector_x, windward.WindVector_z,
          leeward.WindVector_x, leeward.WindVector_z)
end

analysis ConvectionSpeedSweepAnalysis
  extends TransientAnalysis(stop = 200.0)
  model = ConvectionSpeedSweep()
end

"""
Internal-convection sign flip: room air ramped through the surface temperature.

A floor and a ceiling of equal area face air whose temperature ramps from 5 K below the
surface to 5 K above it, crossing at t = 100 s. The pair swaps roles at the crossing — the
floor's coefficient steps up as it starts driving a plume, the ceiling's steps down as it
becomes stably stratified — and `wall.h` stays at the orientation-independent 1.3 K^(1/3)
law throughout.

The crossing is the case upstream's own source comments on as producing initialization
failures: `K·|ΔT|^(1/3)` has an infinite derivative at `ΔT = 0`. The smooth form this port
uses is what the run is checking.
"""
test component InternalConvectionCrossover
  air_temp = BlockComponents.Sources.Ramp(height = 10.0, duration = 200.0, offset = 293.15, start_time = 0.0)
  air = ThermalComponents.Sources.PrescribedTemperature()
  surface = ThermalComponents.Sources.FixedTemperature(T = 298.15)

  floor_surf = InternalConvection(Surf = 12.0, wall_type = WallType.Floor())
  ceil_surf = InternalConvection(Surf = 12.0, wall_type = WallType.Ceil())
  wall_surf = InternalConvection(Surf = 12.0, wall_type = WallType.Wall())
relations
  connect(air_temp.y, air.T)
  connect(surface.port, floor_surf.solid, ceil_surf.solid, wall_surf.solid)
  connect(floor_surf.fluid, ceil_surf.fluid, wall_surf.fluid, air.port)
end

analysis InternalConvectionCrossoverAnalysis
  extends TransientAnalysis(stop = 200.0)
  model = InternalConvectionCrossover()
end
